  <h2>Tillamook Country Smoker Beef Jerky Copycat</h2>
  <p>Tillamook Country Smoker is one of the most popular commercial smoked beef jerky brands. To replicate their signature "Old Fashioned" flavor at home: use a heavier hand of black pepper (1.5 tsp), skip sweeteners entirely, smoke with hickory for 5 hours at 165°F, and add 1/2 teaspoon of liquid smoke to the marinade for extra depth. The result is a dry, peppery, intensely smoky jerky.</p>

  <h2 id="traeger-pellet-grill">Traeger &amp; Pellet Grill Beef Jerky</h2>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p>To make beef jerky on a Traeger or any pellet grill, set the temperature to <strong>165–180°F</strong> and smoke for <strong>4–5 hours</strong>, checking with the bend test from 3.5 hours on. Pellet grills are the easiest smokers for jerky because the auger holds temperature automatically — no fuel management, no babysitting.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Pellet grills changed home jerky making. A stick burner or charcoal smoker demands constant attention to stay in the narrow 160–180°F band jerky needs, and a 20-degree drift for an hour is the difference between dried and half-cooked. A Traeger's auger and controller hold that band on their own for the full session.</p>

  <h3>Traeger Beef Jerky Recipe — Settings</h3>
  <p>Use the <a href="#smoked-beef-jerky-marinade">marinade above</a> — omit the liquid smoke, since the pellets supply real smoke — then:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> 165°F on most Traeger models (the lowest setting on current WiFIRE grills). If your model has a legacy <em>Smoke</em> setting instead of a numeric low temp, that runs roughly 160–180°F and works well.</li>
    <li><strong>Time:</strong> 4–5 hours for 1/4-inch strips. Start the bend test at 3.5 hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Pellets:</strong> apple, cherry, or pecan for mild smoke; hickory or mesquite if you want it assertive.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> lay strips directly across the grates, or use a jerky rack so thin pieces can't fall through. Line the grease tray with foil — drippings are much easier to clean than baked-on marinade.</li>
    <li><strong>Super Smoke:</strong> if your Traeger has it (Timberline, Ironwood, and WiFIRE-equipped Pro models), turn it on. It operates between 165°F and 225°F and increases smoke output noticeably at the low end — exactly the range jerky lives in.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How Long to Smoke Jerky on a Traeger</h3>
  <p>Plan on <strong>4–5 hours at 165°F</strong> for standard 1/4-inch slices. Thicker 3/8-inch strips run 6–7 hours; thin 1/8-inch strips can finish in 3. Pellet grills tend to run slightly drier than a dehydrator because the exhaust continuously vents moist air, so start checking earlier than you would on trays — the first bend test at 3.5 hours, then every 30 minutes.</p>
  <p>Rotate the strips once at the halfway mark. Every pellet grill has a hot side near the firepot, and the strips over it will finish 30–45 minutes ahead of the rest.</p>

  <h3>What Temperature to Smoke Jerky on a Pellet Grill</h3>
  <p>Smoke jerky at <strong>165–180°F</strong>. This is low enough to dehydrate the meat rather than cook it, and high enough to satisfy the USDA's 160°F internal-temperature guidance for jerky safety — see the <a href="/guides/beef-jerky-time-and-temperature/">time and temperature guide</a> for the full breakdown by method.</p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Pellet grill temp</th><th>Time (1/4-inch)</th><th>Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>165°F</td><td>4–5 hours</td><td>✅ Ideal — true drying, maximum smoke contact</td></tr>
      <tr><td>180°F</td><td>3–4 hours</td><td>Good — faster, slightly firmer bite</td></tr>
      <tr><td>200°F</td><td>2–3 hours</td><td>Borderline — starts cooking the meat</td></tr>
      <tr><td>225°F</td><td>1.5–2 hours</td><td>❌ Too hot — see below</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Can You Smoke Jerky at 225°F?</h3>
  <p>You can, but you shouldn't — and it's the most common pellet-grill jerky mistake. 225°F is the default low-and-slow barbecue temperature, so it's the number most people reach for, but it <strong>cooks</strong> the beef instead of dehydrating it. The strips render and firm up before enough moisture has left, which produces a brittle, snappy texture rather than the pliable bend-and-crack you want, and the shorter time on the grill means far less smoke absorbed. If 225°F is genuinely the lowest your grill will go, pull the jerky at 1.5–2 hours and accept a more "cooked" result — or finish the batch in a dehydrator instead.</p>

  <h3>Best Traeger Pellets for Beef Jerky</h3>
  <p>Jerky sits in the smoke for hours with a large surface-area-to-mass ratio, so it absorbs far more smoke per ounce than a brisket does. That makes pellet choice matter more here than almost anywhere else on the grill — err milder than you think you want.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Apple or cherry</strong> — mild and slightly sweet; the safest choice and the best match for teriyaki, honey, and BBQ marinades. Cherry also deepens the color.</li>
    <li><strong>Pecan</strong> — nutty and rich, a middle ground between fruit woods and hickory.</li>
    <li><strong>Hickory</strong> — classic and assertive; excellent with peppered and classic savory marinades, but it can dominate a sweet one.</li>
    <li><strong>Mesquite</strong> — the strongest common option. Over a 5-hour jerky session it turns acrid for most palates. Use it as a blend component rather than on its own.</li>
    <li><strong>Competition/signature blends</strong> — balanced and forgiving; a good default if you only stock one bag.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Other Pellet Smokers: Pit Boss, Camp Chef &amp; Rec Teq</h3>
  <p>Everything above applies to any pellet grill, not just Traeger — the technique is identical, only the lowest available temperature differs. Pit Boss and Camp Chef models typically bottom out around 160–180°F, and many Camp Chef units have a dedicated low smoke setting that is ideal for jerky. Rec Teq grills hold low temperatures unusually tightly, which makes them excellent jerky machines. Check your grill's minimum temperature first: if it won't go below 200°F, you'll get better jerky by smoking for 60–90 minutes for flavor and then finishing in a <a href="/dehydrator-beef-jerky/">dehydrator at 160°F</a>.</p>

  <h3>Traeger Jerky Troubleshooting</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strips are cooked, not dried.</strong> Temperature ran too high — verify with an independent probe thermometer, since controller readings can drift 15–20°F from grate temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Not smoky enough.</strong> Pellet grills produce less smoke at low temps by design. Enable Super Smoke if available, or run the first hour at 180°F before dropping to 165°F.</li>
    <li><strong>Uneven doneness.</strong> Rotate strips at the halfway point; the area above the firepot always runs hotter.</li>
    <li><strong>Strips fell through the grates.</strong> Use a jerky rack, a mesh mat, or lay the strips perpendicular to the bars.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a sweeter strip off the Traeger, the <a href="/recipes/bbq-beef-jerky/">honey BBQ jerky recipe</a> takes hickory smoke especially well — skip its liquid smoke and let the pellets do the work. For heat, the <a href="/recipes/spicy-beef-jerky/">spicy beef jerky recipe</a> pairs with cherry pellets.</p>

  <h2>Electric Smoker Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Electric smokers like the Masterbuilt hold temperature precisely, making them ideal for jerky. Use wood chips in the tray, set to 160°F, and plan for 4–6 hours. Rotate the racks from bottom to top midway through, as electric smokers often run hotter at the bottom.</p>

